  "question": {
    "text": "The most stable trihalide of nitrogen is."
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NI</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The size of halogen increases from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math>&nbsp;to&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math>&nbsp;bond is stronger than other&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">X</mi></math>&nbsp;bonds due to the high electronegativity of fluorine. Larger the size of the halogen, less is the stability of nitrogen halide:</p><p>The stability order is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>NCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>NBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mi>NI</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>.</p>"
  }
